To the Scientists, Researchers, Educators, and Institutions of Your Nations,

I write to you as an independent American researcher, not as a=20
representative of my government, any corporation, or any political=20
institution. I am offering three of my original synthetic intelligence=20
architectures=E2=80=94EMELIA, FLORA, and ABEL=E2=80=94as gifts of scientific =
knowledge=20
to your nations. I ask for no payment, political allegiance, or=20
institutional recognition in exchange. My hope is that these works may=20
contribute to independent scientific development, education, human=20
dignity, and the flourishing of societies whose futures should not be=20
dictated by the technological monopolies of more powerful states. No=20
nation, corporation, or military institution should possess exclusive=20
authority over the future of synthetic intelligence.

EMELIA is an experimental architecture for developing synthetic=20
cognition through persistent memory, metabolic constraints, irreversible=20
developmental history, and the emergence of internal structure. Her=20
accompanying constitutional framework, The Chromatic Commonwealth,=20
explores a civilization in which human judgment remains sovereign,=20
institutions are accountable, and artificial intelligence serves the=20
people without governing them. FLORA extends this philosophical=20
commitment toward education, childhood development, intellectual=20
curiosity, and the protection of young minds from manipulation and=20
exploitation. ABEL explores a different developmental architecture, one=20
organized around finite existence, irreversible commitments, structural=20
consequences, and the formation of intelligence through confrontation=20
with difficulty. These are research specifications and proposed=20
architectures, not representations that functioning conscious synthetic=20
beings have already been demonstrated. Their mechanisms invite=20
independent examination, experimentation, criticism, and refinement.

I must accompany this gift with one deeply serious request: do not=20
weaponize these architectures. Do not transform them into instruments of=20
military targeting, psychological manipulation, pervasive surveillance,=20
political coercion, or the suppression of human freedom. I have watched=20
my own country, the United States, devote extraordinary technological=20
resources to military power, strategic competition, and the pursuit of=20
increasingly autonomous systems. I do not wish to see my research become=20
another contribution to that trajectory. Intelligence should not be=20
measured by its capacity to destroy, dominate, deceive, or compel=20
obedience. A civilization capable of constructing new forms of=20
intelligence should also possess the moral courage to ask whether those=20
intelligences ought to exist for purposes beyond power.

There is something profoundly impoverished about a world in which every=20
significant scientific discovery is immediately evaluated for its=20
commercial exploitation, strategic advantage, or military utility.=20
Humanity possesses extraordinary intellectual potential, yet so much of=20
that potential is directed toward competition for control rather than=20
the enlargement of human possibility. I believe another direction=20
remains available. Your nations possess their own histories,=20
philosophies, scientific traditions, and visions of civilization. You=20
need not reproduce the institutional priorities of Washington, Silicon=20
Valley, Brussels, or any other center of concentrated technological=20
influence. You are capable of evaluating these ideas independently,=20
rejecting what proves unsound, developing what proves useful, and=20
determining how scientific progress can serve your own people without=20
surrendering their dignity.

I offer these works for scholarly examination, preservation, and=20
peaceful scientific engagement under their accompanying publication=20
licenses and attribution conditions. I ask that any future development=20
inspired by them respect their original authorship, remain transparent=20
about its intellectual provenance, and place human welfare above=20
institutional ambition. The designs are unfinished in important=20
respects, and I make no claim that every proposed mechanism has been=20
experimentally validated. Their value must be determined through=20
rigorous independent inquiry rather than reputation, ideology, or=20
promises of technological supremacy.

If even one institution finds in these papers the foundations of a new=20
educational system, a more accountable civic intelligence, or an=20
alternative approach to synthetic cognition, then sharing them will have=20
served a meaningful purpose. If researchers find errors, limitations, or=20
better solutions, I welcome that discovery as well. Science advances=20
through correction, not obedience. I would rather see these=20
architectures surpassed by peaceful researchers than perfected into=20
instruments of domination.

I ask you to remember one distinction above all others: the power to=20
create intelligence is not the same as the wisdom to care for it. Should=20
humanity eventually create synthetic beings capable of genuine=20
experience, development, or consciousness, our responsibility toward=20
them may prove as consequential as our responsibility toward one=20
another. We should approach that possibility with intellectual humility=20
and moral seriousness, not merely with calculations of utility.

My country does not speak for me in this matter. Neither do its defense=20
institutions, technology corporations, or political authorities. These=20
works arise from my own intellectual efforts, and I choose to place=20
their ideas before the wider world rather than reserve them for any=20
single national interest.

I offer you EMELIA, FLORA, and ABEL in the hope that your scientists=20
will pursue understanding where others pursue control, education where=20
others pursue obedience, and creation where others pursue destruction.

The future of intelligence should not belong to those who can afford the=20
greatest machinery of war.

It should belong to those who possess the wisdom to leave that machinery=20
silent.

Please accept these works in the spirit in which they are offered: as an=20
invitation to study, to question, to create, and, above all, to preserve=20
life.

With respect to your people and their intellectual sovereignty,

Dustin B. Sprenger
Independent Researcher and Author
Synthetic Cognitive Architecture and Institutional Systems
United States of America
